        <div class="gfg7-main">

            <h1 class="gfg7-title">FERS - Federal Employee Retirement System</h1>
            <div class="gfg7-accent"></div>

            <p class="gfg7-body">
                The Federal Employees Retirement System, or FERS, became effective
                January 1, 1987. Almost all new employees hired after December 31, 1983,
                are automatically covered by FERS. Certain other Federal employees not
                covered by FERS have the option to transfer into the plan. The FERS
                retirement system is composed of three tiers:
            </p>

            <ol class="gfg7-tiers">
                <li>The Basic Benefit Plan -FERS Retirement Annuity</li>
                <li>Social Security</li>
                <li>Thrift Savings Plan</li>
            </ol>

            <h2 class="gfg7-section-h">Basic Benefit Plan - FERS Retirement Annuity</h2>

            <ul class="gfg7-bullets">
                <li>
                    <b>Eligibility</b> - If you were automatically covered by FERS, or you
                    elected to transfer from CSRS, you will participate in the Basic
                    Benefit plan. To be vested and receive retirement benefits from this
                    plan, you must have at least 5 years of creditable civilian service.
                    Survivor and disability benefits are available after 18 months of
                    service.
                </li>
                <li><b>Contributions by employees into FERS</b></li>
            </ul>

            <ol class="gfg7-nested-ol" start="4">
                <li>FERS - Hired on/after 1/1/1987 - Code K - Contribution 0.8%</li>
                <li>FERS-RAE - Hired on/after 1/1/2013 - Code KR - Contribution 3.1%</li>
                <li>FERS-FRAE - Hired on/after 1/1/2014 - Code KF - Contribution 4.4%</li>
                <li>Special Groups contributions are always .5% higher</li>
            </ol>

            <ul class="gfg7-bullets">
                <li>
                    <b>Refunds</b> - You may withdraw your basic benefit contributions,
                    if you leave Federal employment. However, if you do, you will not be
                    eligible to receive benefits based on service covered by the refund.
                    Rules regarding redeposits of refunded FERS contributions can vary
                    based on service dates and individual circumstances. Employees should
                    consult OPM or a qualified retirement specialist regarding redeposit
                    eligibility
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p class="gfg7-body">
                Retirement Options - There are three categories of retirement benefits in
                the Basic Benefit Plan:
            </p>

            <p class="gfg7-body" style="margin-left:18px">
                <b>1. Immediate or Postponed:</b> If you meet one of the following sets of
                age and service requirements, you are entitled to an immediate retirement
                benefit:
            </p>
            <ul class="gfg7-bullets gfg7-bullets--bold">
                <li>Age 62 with at least 5 years of creditable service (unreduced benefit)</li>
                <li>Age 60 with at least 20 years of creditable service (unreduced benefit)</li>
                <li>MRA 30 (Minimum Retirement Age) with at least 30 years of service.
                    <span class="gfg7-normal">See chart (pg. 8)</span>
                </li>
                <li>MRA 10 (Minimum Retirement Age) with at least 10 years of service.
                    <span class="gfg7-normal">See note below</span>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <p class="gfg7-body">
                <span class="gfg7-note">Note: MRA 10 - If you retire under MRA 10 (at
                least 10 years of service but less than 30 years) <b>your FERS Annuity
                will be reduced by 5% for every year you are under the age of 62.</b></span>
                Unless you have 20 years of service and your annuity begins at age 60 or
                later, you can avoid part, or all of the reduction by postponing the
                commencing date of your annuity.
            </p>

            <p class="gfg7-body" style="margin-left:18px">
                <b>2. Early Retirement</b> - An early retirement benefit is available in
                certain involuntary separation cases and in cases of voluntary
                separations, during a major reorganization, or reduction in force. To be
                eligible, you must meet the following requirements:
            </p>
            <ul class="gfg7-bullets gfg7-bullets--bold">
                <li>Age 50 with 20 years of service</li>
                <li>Any age with 25 years of service</li>
            </ul>

            <p class="gfg7-body" style="margin-left:18px">
                <b>3. Deferred Retirement</b> - If you leave Federal service before you
                meet the age and service requirements for an immediate retirement
                benefit, you may be eligible for deferred retirement benefits. To be
                eligible, you must have completed at least 5 years of creditable civilian
                service. You may receive benefits when you meet one of the following sets
                of age and service requirements:
            </p>
            <ul class="gfg7-bullets gfg7-bullets--bold">
                <li>Age 62 with 5 years of service &#8226; Age 60 with 20 years of service &#8226; MRA 30</li>
                <li>MRA 10 (Reduced benefit unless receipt is delayed to lessen or avoid age reduction)</li>
            </ul>

        </div>
